34,2,1,talk,Toshio,,"The data suggests a clear trend: projects that offer flexible, bookable spaces for the whole campus are far more successful and in higher demand than those serving limited, specific areas.","{""words"":30,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
35,2,1,talk,Victoria,,"My findings support that. We've faced significant costs hiring off-site space when rooms are out of service, and there is a desperate, unmet demand for general-purpose rooms compared to idle specialist ones.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
36,2,1,talk,George,,It seems our technicians are spending far too much time on manual tasks that modern equipment should handle. We must consider how our choices affect the daily workload of the staff.,"{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
37,2,1,talk,Mizuki,,"It is concerning that projects based on comfort or appeal often slip by a year. Furthermore, hazardous materials in older ground-floor areas have caused significant delays, which we must account for in any new plan.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
38,2,1,talk,Benjamin,,"It appears our financial forecasts are optimistic fantasies. Mechanical costs are climbing sharply, and we have neglected to fund the replacement of our audiovisual assets. We must be wary of any plan with high technical overhead.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
39,2,2,talk,Toshio,,"Given the high demand for general-purpose space and the failure of narrow projects, we should avoid any plan that benefits only a single wing or a specific department.","{""words"":28,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
40,2,2,talk,Victoria,,"We must also consider the operational cost of the construction phase. If a project requires taking rooms out of service, the expense of hiring external space could easily undermine the budget.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
41,2,2,talk,George,,"If we can find a project that automates those tedious manual routines, we would provide much-needed relief to our technicians who have been struggling for years.","{""words"":26,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
42,2,2,talk,Mizuki,,"If we choose a project in an older ground-floor area, we risk hazardous material delays. We should prioritize plans that are based on inspection findings rather than mere appeal to ensure they stay on schedule.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
43,2,2,talk,Benjamin,,"Precisely. If we ignore the rising cost of mechanical plant and the void in our renewal reserves, we are merely painting a pretty picture of a bankruptcy. Logic dictates we avoid expensive technical traps.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
44,2,3,talk,Toshio,,It seems we are balancing a need for general-use flexibility against the risks of technical costs and delays. We should look for a candidate that maximizes campus-wide access while minimizing specialized mechanical risks.,"{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
45,2,3,talk,Victoria,,"I agree. We need a solution that addresses the shortage of general-purpose rooms without incurring the hidden costs of off-site rentals or the risks associated with complex, specialized installations.","{""words"":29,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
46,2,3,talk,George,,"I believe we should prioritize a project that reduces the manual burden on our staff. If a plan can replace outdated equipment that requires constant manual intervention, it is a win for the university.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
47,2,3,talk,Mizuki,,"We must be cautious. Any project that is purely for ""appeal"" or located in older ground-floor sections is a recipe for delays. We need a plan that is practically viable and timely.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
48,2,3,talk,Benjamin,,"It is a delicate dance, isn't it? We seek the ideal intersection of utility and feasibility, while avoiding the siren song of projects that look splendid on a brochure but bleed us dry in practice.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
49,2,4,talk,Toshio,,"I will maintain my focus on the data. We need a project that is bookable by all and avoids the pitfalls of narrow utility, as that is where the highest satisfaction lies.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
50,2,4,talk,Victoria,,I will prioritize a plan that provides general-purpose utility and minimizes the need for costly off-site room replacements during construction. We must ensure the chosen facility is actually accessible to the wider campus community.,"{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
51,2,4,talk,George,,I will stand by the need to support our staff. Any project that replaces obsolete systems and removes the burden of manual routines is the only way to ensure our operations remain sustainable.,"{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
52,2,4,talk,Mizuki,,"I will prioritize a project that is likely to be delivered on time. We must avoid ground-floor risks and ""appeal-based"" projects, focusing instead on those that address essential inspection findings or operational necessities.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
53,2,4,talk,Benjamin,,I shall remain the voice of fiscal caution. We must avoid any project that ignores the reality of rising mechanical costs or relies on non-existent reserves for high-tech equipment. Let us see which candidates survive this scrutiny.,"{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
