59,3,1,talk,Toshio,,"Candidate B offers the bookable, general-purpose space that the data shows is most valued. However, Candidate D also provides campus-wide access. I lean toward B for its broader utility.","{""words"":29,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
60,3,1,talk,Victoria,,"Candidate B provides the general-purpose space we desperately need, but it is on the ground floor, which Mizuki warned about. Candidate D is bookable and avoids those specific structural risks.","{""words"":30,""mentions"":[""Mizuki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
61,3,1,talk,George,,"Candidate A is the only option that directly addresses the outdated extraction equipment. Replacing it would eliminate the manual routines currently burdening our technicians, providing them with long-overdue relief.","{""words"":29,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
62,3,1,talk,Mizuki,,"Candidate B is a ground-floor project, which means a high risk of hazardous material delays. Candidate D is for ""appeal,"" which often slips. Candidate A addresses essential equipment, making it the most viable for timely delivery.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
63,3,1,talk,Benjamin,,Candidate A is a mechanical nightmare given the rising costs of ducted plant. Candidate D relies on AV kit we cannot afford to replace. Candidate C is fiscally stable and avoids these technical traps.,"{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
64,3,2,talk,Toshio,,"While Candidate C is safe, it offers no bookable utility. Candidate B's ground-floor risk is real, but the demand for general-purpose space is overwhelming. I believe the benefit of B outweighs the risk.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
65,3,2,talk,Victoria,,"If we choose B, we risk the ground-floor delays and the cost of off-site rentals. Candidate D provides the bookable utility we need while avoiding those specific pitfalls. It seems the most balanced choice.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,2,talk,George,,I cannot ignore the plight of the staff. Candidate A is the only plan that solves a concrete operational failure. We cannot keep expecting our technicians to perform manual work that machines should do.,"{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,2,talk,Mizuki,,Candidate A is the only one that aligns with inspection-style necessity rather than mere appeal. It avoids the ground-floor hazards of B and the scheduling instability of D. It is the most reliable choice.,"{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,2,talk,Benjamin,,"It is fascinating how we diverge. George and Mizuki see a necessity in A, yet they ignore that ""necessity"" now costs twenty percent more than planned. I shall stick to the path of least resistance: Candidate C.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""George"",""Mizuki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,3,talk,Toshio,,"If we prioritize the users, Candidate B is the strongest. The data on post-occupancy ratings is clear: general-purpose, bookable spaces are the only ones that truly land well with the campus community.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
70,3,3,talk,Victoria,,I must disagree with B. The cost of hiring off-site space during a delayed ground-floor renovation would be prohibitive. Candidate D provides the bookable utility we need without those specific operational risks.,"{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
71,3,3,talk,George,,"The technicians cannot wait any longer. Candidate A is not about ""appeal""; it is about basic operational functionality. We must stop sacrificing our staff's well-being for the sake of a few bookable rooms.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
72,3,3,talk,Mizuki,,"Candidate A is the only project that avoids the ""appeal"" trap and the ground-floor hazards. It is a matter of operational necessity, ensuring we actually deliver the project on schedule.","{""words"":30,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
73,3,3,talk,Benjamin,,"We are essentially choosing between a staff-saving mechanical project that will likely exceed its budget, a high-demand space that may never be finished, or a simple path. I choose Candidate C.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
74,3,4,talk,Toshio,,"I will vote for Candidate B. Despite the risks, the data proves that general-purpose, bookable spaces provide the highest value and satisfaction for the university community. It is the only choice that truly meets user demand.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
75,3,4,talk,Victoria,,"I will vote for Candidate D. It satisfies the urgent need for bookable, general-purpose utility while avoiding the ground-floor hazards and the prohibitive costs of off-site room rentals associated with Candidate B.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
76,3,4,talk,George,,I will vote for Candidate A. It is the only plan that addresses the operational failures and provides essential relief to our overburdened staff. We must prioritize the people who keep this university running.,"{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
77,3,4,talk,Mizuki,,I will vote for Candidate A. It is the only project that addresses a functional necessity and avoids the ground-floor risks and scheduling instability of the other options. It is the most reliable choice.,"{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
78,3,4,talk,Benjamin,,"I shall cast my vote for Candidate C. While others chase high-risk utilities or expensive mechanical dreams, I prefer a plan that actually respects the budget and the laws of physics.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
